Tokens: 1.2k sent, 429 received.
Applied edit to PhoneNumber.java
response:
Ok, I will update the `PhoneNumber.java` file to implement the logic for cleaning up phone numbers according to the NANP rules.

Here is the plan:
1.  In the constructor `PhoneNumber(String numberString)`, I will process the input string to remove all non-digit characters.
2.  I will check if the resulting string has a valid length (10 or 11 digits).
3.  If it has 11 digits, I will check if the first digit is '1'. If not, it's invalid. If it is '1', I will strip the leading '1' to keep only the 10-digit NANP number.
4.  If the length is not 10 or 11, or if an 11-digit number doesn't start with '1', I will throw an `IllegalArgumentException`.
5.  I will store the cleaned 10-digit number in a private field.
6.  The `getNumber()` method will return this stored cleaned number.

PhoneNumber.java
```java
class PhoneNumber {

    private String number;

    PhoneNumber(String numberString) {
        // Remove all non-digit characters
        String cleaned = numberString.replaceAll("\\D", "");

        if (cleaned.length() == 11) {
            if (cleaned.charAt(0) != '1') {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid country code");
            }
            cleaned = cleaned.substring(1);
        }

        if (cleaned.length() != 10) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid phone number length");
        }

        // Validate area code and exchange code (must be 2-9)
        if (cleaned.charAt(0) < '2' || cleaned.charAt(3) < '2') {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid area code or exchange code");
        }

        this.number = cleaned;
    }

    String getNumber() {
        return number;
    }

}
```
